  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 249 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An occluding low will track well to the north today into \nWednesday, bringing several rounds of showers. A few showers will \nmove through this morning, but more widespread showers and \nthunderstorms will move through in the afternoon and evening. They \nwill mostly dissipate and move out tonight, before another round of \nshowers and thunderstorms develops on Wednesday, though mostly for \nVermont. Right now, the severe threat is relatively low. There does \nnot look to be a well-defined boundary; instead several rounds of \nshowers with plenty of clouds will move through. There is very \nlittle time for solar heating and instability to form, and the \nairmass will be relatively cool to start with. With the low tracking \nwell to the north, the best synoptic forcing will be well to the \nnorth, though there will be adequate wind shear. Overall, the region \nis in an SPC marginal risk which seems representative. While an \nisolated strong to severe storm is possible, that currently looks to \nbe the extent of it, though the amount of heating will still be \nwatched as more sun than expected could increase storm coverage a \nlittle. The system does not look to provide much of a flooding \nthreat either. HREF probabilities of receiving more than an inch of \nrain are generally between 10-40 percent. While there will be areas \nof convective enhancement, the storms will be quick-moving and there \nwill be gaps between the showers. PWATs should rise into the 1.5-\n1.75 inch range but with the expected setup, that should not pose \ntoo much of a problem. While limited wildfire smoke may return \ntoday, its concentration will be significantly less than on Saturday \nand it will likely not have much of an effect on the convection.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A seasonably cool air mass, with minimal high altitude \nsmoke, should make for outstanding weather for outdoor activities on \nThursday and Friday as an upper level trough pushes to our \nnortheast. Westerly flow in the morning will probably support some \nrough conditions on Lake Champlain that should improve during the \nday as anomalous low level temperatures moderate along with \ndecreasing wind speeds. Winds decrease significantly for Friday. \nExpect high temperatures not too far off of seasonable norms due to \nabundant sunshine, currently projected to range through the 70s and \ninto the lower 80s. Lows at night will be in the 40s and 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: High pressure will dominate this weekend, resulting in \ndry weather Friday night into Sunday. This is also allow for above \nseasonable temperatures with highs in the 80s and lows in the 50s to \nmid 60s as southwesterly flow aloft increases, advecting in warmer \nair ahead of our next system. This next system is expected to swing \nthrough early next week featuring a trough and cold front. This far \nout, models show significant differences from each other as well as \nfrom run to run, but most likely period of showers and thunderstorms \nwould be Monday. This should also knock temperatures back down \ntowards normal in the 70s to mid 80 and lows in the upper 40s to \nlower 60s.",
